 "script": [
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "In eight ninety-seven, a pope put a dead man on trial. Not a painting of him. His actual corpse."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "And no, this ain't a horror movie. It's in the history books. They call it the Cadaver Synod."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Here's the deal. Rome. Pope Formosus has been dead for about nine months. The new pope, Stephen the Sixth, really did not like him."
  },
  {
   "beat": "dig",
   "text": "So what does he do? He digs him up. Dresses the body in full papal robes. And sits it on a throne, in front of a room full of bishops."
  },
  {
   "beat": "trial",
   "text": "Now, a corpse can't talk. So they gave it a lawyer. A young deacon stood behind the throne and answered for the dead guy."
  },
  {
   "beat": "trial",
   "text": "The charge? Breaking church rules to become pope. And you know what the verdict was? Guilty."
  },
  {
   "beat": "after",
   "text": "Stephen cut off the three fingers Formosus used for blessings and threw the body in the River Tiber. Like taking out the trash."
  },
  {
   "beat": "turn",
   "text": "But Rome had seen enough. Within months the people turned on Stephen. He was thrown in prison, and strangled."
  },
  {
   "beat": "turn",
   "text": "And Formosus? Pulled out of the river by a monk, and buried again, back in Saint Peter's."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "That's real."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "So next time you think your boss holds a grudge, at least he isn't digging you up."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "That's about it, folks. Follow for more strange history. See ya!"
  }
 ],
 "science": [
  {
   "claim": "In January 897 Pope Stephen VI had the exhumed corpse of Pope Formosus (died April 896) dressed in papal vestments, seated on a throne and tried before a synod at the Lateran; a deacon answered for the dead pope; Formosus was found guilty (of perjury and of serving as pope illegally, having been a bishop of another see), his acts were annulled, the three fingers of his right hand used for blessings were cut off, and the body was thrown into the Tiber (after a brief burial in a common grave); it was recovered (by a monk / hermit, according to the accounts) and later reburied in St Peter's by Theodore II; a popular uprising in Rome deposed Stephen VI, who was imprisoned and strangled in the summer of 897.",
   "working": "",
   "source": "Wikipedia, Cadaver Synod; Liutprand of Cremona, Antapodosis; Encyclopaedia Britannica"
  }
 ],
